you know, to be able to spend time with him,

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:28.460
get his input in our show,

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:30.240
have him think with us the show.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:32.640
And I was always wondering what did we give him?

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:34.620
And I think in a way, I think for him,

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.660
it was very liberating that I had nothing to do

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:38.740
with the architecture world and that, you know,

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:41.420
he was put in other repair, into other references.

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:42.460
And there were all of a sudden,

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:45.160
he was in an exhibition with a hundred artists,

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.900
visual artists from China and, you know,

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:50.540
and Southeast Asia and Japan.

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:53.780
And he found that very amusing and much more amusing

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:56.420
than to be in an architecture biennale, which he hated.

00:12:56.420 --> 00:13:00.396
I think there is a wonderful text by Rem Koolhaas, you know, which Rem, we asked

00:13:00.396 --> 00:13:02.980
Rem, whom Cedric taught, Cedric was Rem's professor,

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:09.235
we asked Rem to write for the RECP book, for the book I edited, and we asked

00:13:09.235 --> 00:13:10.820
actually Isozaki and,

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:16.780
why not, we asked Isozaki and Rem and also Patrick Keeler to write.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:22.812
Isozaki revisited his text, you know, which he wrote early on, where he introduced

00:13:22.812 --> 00:13:23.820
Cedric to Asia,

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:28.246
to Asia and Rem wrote a very short text but very compressed where he talks about

00:13:28.246 --> 00:13:29.020
this idea that

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:33.936
Cedric really changed architecture with so few means but that actually these

00:13:33.936 --> 00:13:35.100
lapidary epigrams,

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:40.630
these skeletal drawings and also as Rem says his polemical genius for mobilizing the

00:13:40.630 --> 00:13:41.420
real against

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:46.051
the pretensions of a still surprisingly megalomaniac profession, that's he changed

00:13:46.051 --> 00:13:46.220
the

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:51.447
terrain through this in the 60s and Rem calls him a puritanical Oscar Wilde. It's a

00:13:51.447 --> 00:13:52.140
great text.

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:58.192
he sort of said, you know, Cedric used the ridicule and humor to devastating effect

00:13:58.192 --> 00:14:04.171
to dismantle one by one the most holy ambitions of an unquestioned profession, the

00:14:04.171 --> 00:14:06.140
profession of architecture.

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:10.431
And in a way, it's also part of an extraordinary London, an extraordinary history

00:14:10.431 --> 00:14:14.140
where you basically have, of course, Denise Cobb Brown, the Smithsons,

00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:20.448
And of course Cedric were sort of all connected to a fascination for the ordinary,

00:14:20.448 --> 00:14:21.140
which was

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:25.140
what the Independent Group was about, but also what connected it to Cedric.

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:33.140
And in a way, that actually an attention to the real, the raw and the ugly,

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:38.140
is I think something Rem found very inspiring in Cedric.

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:41.140
There are so many aspects of where Cedric can be a toolbox.

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:45.647
For me, personally, being a curator and a museum director, the great toolbox is

00:14:45.647 --> 00:14:46.160
obviously

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:47.720
thinking of museums.

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:51.940
We did together the Nano Museum, which is my portable museum.

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:55.961
I found it in the 90s, which is a little frame, two by three inches, where the

00:14:55.961 --> 00:14:56.580
museum could

00:14:56.580 --> 00:14:57.580
go anywhere.

00:14:57.580 --> 00:14:59.860
The museum would be portable.

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:04.200
Douglas Gordon lost the museum in the pub in Glasgow, so the museum actually died.

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:06.360
You have here a photo of the museum.

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:07.360
It's a Nano Museum.

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:12.340
an exhibition in that where he really defined what the museum for the 21st

00:15:12.340 --> 00:15:18.880
century could be. He measured flexibility, he developed

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:23.120
a whole new set of rules of what the museum could be when we developed this

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:26.380
nano museum together. I think for me many of his other ideas like the

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:31.620
museum on a boat, on a cruise ship, the Fun Palace of course as a cultural

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:34.960
center which would not be a place for objects but a place for intensities, a

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:39.560
a place where all intensities could kind of meet, remain very very valid.

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:43.257
But I think somebody who is interested in completely different things will always

00:15:43.257 --> 00:15:43.440
find

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:44.700
inspiration from Cedric.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:47.960
So we wanted this to be like a recipe book.

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:52.774
And the problem obviously was that Cedric forbid us to do this unless it had an

00:15:52.774 --> 00:15:53.140
expiry

00:15:53.140 --> 00:15:54.140
date.

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:58.058
So the publisher had to agree that actually the book has to best be consumed before

00:15:58.058 --> 00:15:58.200
May

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:03.620
the 1st, 2006, because by this date the author may have changed his mind.

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:07.380
So that was a very important aspect of a paradox somehow of doing a book with him.

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:09.996
Because I think that's particularly important if you think about books on Cedric

00:16:09.996 --> 00:16:10.160
Nile.

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:14.745
We should always keep in mind that he deeply, deeply mistrusted this idea of it

00:16:14.745 --> 00:16:15.500
being frozen.

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:20.519
I think the idea, it's not only the idea of enabling, it's also I think a good hint

00:16:20.519 --> 00:16:20.640
we

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:22.460
can find in the project magnets.

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:26.060
Because I mean magnets in Asia, they're used to heal.

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:29.580
There is a whole healing thing with magnets.

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:33.381
And so it's not necessarily, if you think, talks about architectural magnets, it's

00:16:33.381 --> 00:16:33.520
not

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:37.503
necessarily adding new buildings to cities, but these magnets could actually

00:16:37.503 --> 00:16:38.080
renegotiate

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:43.060
the existing substance, maybe heal it, maybe repoll it, give it a different kind of

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:43.840
significance.

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:48.048
And he had this idea, which he told me very often, that he wanted to add magnets to

00:16:48.048 --> 00:16:48.200
the

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:51.700
city to re-dynamize it, to reinvent it.

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:56.738
And I think that sort of whole idea that we can actually, we live in an age of more

00:16:56.738 --> 00:16:56.920
and

00:16:56.920 --> 00:17:00.840
more limited resources and I think it's not necessarily about permanently adding new

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:01.260
buildings

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:04.626
but maybe also thinking about what we could do with the existing and how the

00:17:04.626 --> 00:17:04.980
existing

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:09.275
could actually be redefined. That's what the idea of the US found is I think another

00:17:09.275 --> 00:17:09.480
kind

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:13.140
of, it's something we discuss a lot in Venice.

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:16.344
For me it will always be the front palace because it's closest to my heart because

00:17:16.344 --> 00:17:16.500
I've

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:21.487
always felt what is missing and it's interesting, I mean Ernst Bloch when pushed

00:17:21.487 --> 00:17:22.360
hard by Adorno

00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:27.030
in a conversation about what would be utopia, he defined utopia as something that's

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:27.480
missing.

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:32.615
And I still think if we think what's missing in our time, it is these

00:17:32.615 --> 00:17:33.880
pluridisciplinary

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:37.145
institutions because bigger institutions, whenever they wanted to bring all the

00:17:37.145 --> 00:17:37.600
disciplines

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:42.304
together, all the different art forms together, it then again became

00:17:42.304 --> 00:17:43.480
departmentalized.

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:48.168
And I think this idea of having an institution or an institute where you wouldn't

00:17:48.168 --> 00:17:48.920
have objects,

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:52.918
you'd have it away, nothing would be frozen, but you would have a situation where

00:17:52.918 --> 00:17:53.560
the different

00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:57.428
– the word discipline is kind of ugly because it means that there are disciplines or

00:17:57.428 --> 00:17:57.980
disciplinary

00:17:57.980 --> 00:18:02.027
boundaries so we'd rather talk maybe about intensities. So in a way, a structure

00:18:02.027 --> 00:18:02.280
where

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:05.787
all these different intensities could encounter, where you have fast lanes and slow

00:18:05.787 --> 00:18:06.040
lanes,

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:10.287
where you have silent zones and noisy zones, I think remains for me the very potent

00:18:10.287 --> 00:18:10.440
and

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:14.850
powerful idea, particularly it was the dialogue between Cedric as an architect and

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:15.280
urbanist

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:19.254
and John Littlewood as a theatre director. And then an amazing think tank like

00:18:19.254 --> 00:18:19.560
Yehudi

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:25.760
Menuhin from music like Gordon Parske for cybernetics. And I think in a way they got

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:29.487
quite close to building it. I think the history of museums, art centres would have

00:18:29.487 --> 00:18:29.760
been a

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:34.111
different one had that project gone ahead. And for me it remains certainly my

00:18:34.111 --> 00:18:34.620
favourite

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:37.688
project of Cedric. But there is also the pottery think-bath which are close to my

00:18:37.688 --> 00:18:38.180
heart because

00:18:38.180 --> 00:18:42.888
I think besides inventing this sort of art centre of intensities which we need for

00:18:42.888 --> 00:18:43.060
the

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:47.208
I think there is also a missing school and what would a Black Mountain College for

00:18:47.208 --> 00:18:48.220
the 21st century be?

00:18:48.220 --> 00:18:51.680
It would probably be on wheels, eventually not on wheels but certainly mobile.

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:55.520
And I think this whole idea that he wanted to use with the Potteries Thinkbelt,

00:18:56.020 --> 00:18:59.636
an abandoned railway line in Staffordshire in England and basically revitalize, like

00:18:59.636 --> 00:19:02.520
with the magnets, you know, where the magnets can heal but can also

00:19:02.740 --> 00:19:07.103
re-dynamize. He wanted to re-dynamize this abandoned railway line and make it into a

00:19:07.103 --> 00:19:09.700
classroom on the move. It's a very beautiful idea.

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:13.841
Yeah, I mean it's in the world of the Fun Palace. It's the idea that he, you know,

00:19:13.841 --> 00:19:14.700
it was a delight.

00:19:14.700 --> 00:19:19.700
And for me, it's among the happiest moments is to experience a morning with Cedric.

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:23.849
It was just about the pure delight, the delight of being alive, the delight of

00:19:23.849 --> 00:19:24.700
inventing ideas,

00:19:24.700 --> 00:19:28.700
the delight of being so free. It was his incredible freedom, you know, he had,

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:33.700
of not having any kind of constraints to his practice. He was free.

00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:36.700
Like the birds in the aviary, I mean, it was always his idea.

00:19:36.700 --> 00:19:42.200
it's almost like a metaphor for his life and for his work, is his idea of not

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:43.700
imprisoning practice.

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:48.888
I think in a way, if you look at the aviary, one of the great masterpieces which he

00:19:48.888 --> 00:19:50.700
built with Newby and Snowdon,

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:57.313
the aviary in the London Zoo, is initially actually destined to fly out of the zoo

00:19:57.313 --> 00:20:00.700
according to the mood swings of the birds.

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:04.700
So should the birds decide they could fly away with the aviary.

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:07.080
So it's the anti-Zoo so to say.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:12.690
And in a way it was then only later that he, you know, in a second round so to say,

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:12.960
that

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:17.115
he agreed that the aviary would actually be fixed, but that the upper parts would

00:20:17.115 --> 00:20:17.320
move

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:21.128
in the wind, you could potentially imagine that the birds can move, it was kind of

00:20:21.128 --> 00:20:21.360
like,

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:23.740
you know, a flexible architecture.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:26.549
But if you think about this, it actually became very important for the Swiss

00:20:26.549 --> 00:20:27.140
pavilion because

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:32.019
what could we do with the pavilion to keep this spirit of the birds being able to

00:20:32.019 --> 00:20:32.200
fly

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:33.200
away.

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:40.409
a bow-wow designed on the roof a kind of aviary for the Swiss Pavilion, but it had

00:20:40.409 --> 00:20:41.200
no glass,

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:45.910
it had no vitrine, so basically there were no birds inside because the birds in any

00:20:45.910 --> 00:20:46.080
way

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:47.360
had flown out.

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:52.031
So the visitor was inside and could hear the birds, and the birds were in the tree,

00:20:52.031 --> 00:20:52.200
the

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:53.200
birds were free.

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:58.030
And I think that was for me almost like perfect for a bow-wow, to do that as an

00:20:58.030 --> 00:20:58.580
homage to

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:03.580
because it was the fun palace, it was what the aviary was always meant to be.

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:06.580
And I think this idea also of not...

00:21:06.580 --> 00:21:10.580
because very often I think, and you see that also with exhibition spaces,

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:15.580
that very often it can be actually an imposition in which someone has to operate.

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:19.580
And Cedric wanted rather this idea of delight to always be there.

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:23.580
And it could be with a building or without a building,

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:26.580
but it could very often be that the building would spoil that delight.

00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:29.460
So, and I think that's a very important part.

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:31.100
And also this idea of play, I think,

00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:32.040
goes hand in hand with this.

00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:33.860
I mean, Robert Louis Stevenson once said,

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:35.880
"'Art is like when children play,

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:37.960
"'but it has the seriousness of playing children.'"

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:40.080
That's how I always felt in the presence of Cedric.

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:43.500
Okay, so once on a rainy Wednesday morning,

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:45.300
I arrived there at seven and had a coffee

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:48.020
and some ex-dancers waiting for him to arrive.

00:21:48.020 --> 00:21:51.240
And at 7.30, we would start the meeting

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:56.240
and he would be compelled to talk about his idea

00:21:56.240 --> 00:22:00.840
of having a city, having a part of the city where umbrellas would pop up.

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:03.840
I don't know what that project is called, I think we have it in the book.

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:10.599
It's a project where all of a sudden, you know, when it rains the umbrellas would go

00:22:10.599 --> 00:22:10.840
up.

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:16.307
So we talked about this idea with Cedric at 7.30, because it was a rainy Wednesday

00:22:16.307 --> 00:22:16.840
morning.

00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:20.333
We talked about the idea that actually he had suggested that streets could be

00:22:20.333 --> 00:22:21.240
covered by umbrellas

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:25.240
and they would pop up when it rains and then he wouldn't have to own an umbrella.

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:27.800
The umbrella would be for everyone, it would be a collective umbrella.

00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:30.746
I mean it's very much also this idea of Buckwheat and Sofulla that we don't

00:22:30.746 --> 00:22:32.200
necessarily have to own architecture,

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:35.000
that we don't have to own buildings, that we don't have to own a car,

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:38.600
that it's more like a service as Buckwheat and Sofulla said.

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:43.640
So he had this idea of the umbrella as an urban service and then all of a sudden,

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:48.231
out of the blue in his office produced a beautifully stripped kind of umbrella which

00:22:48.231 --> 00:22:49.160
matched his shirt

00:22:49.160 --> 00:22:50.680
and started to open it.

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:54.346
So I filmed him whilst he opened the umbrella, whilst obviously we were inside, it

00:22:54.346 --> 00:22:55.240
was raining outside.

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:59.251
So it was like a performance and we then started to talk about the Serge Ranson's

00:22:59.251 --> 00:23:01.120
museum because I told him that, you know,

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:02.360
that's why I came to see him.

00:23:02.360 --> 00:23:05.240
But obviously before I could even start to talk about the project,

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:09.000
he had sort of kidnapped me on this idea of the umbrella because of the rain.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:12.231
And so it was always very, very, you know, there were not just meetings where we

00:23:12.231 --> 00:23:13.120
talked about projects,

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:14.640
it was like a flanerie, it was a stroll.

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:16.920
It's like I want to walk with him, I walk through his ideas.

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:19.560
And he was obviously documented through these magical boxes.

00:23:19.560 --> 00:23:23.560
The boxes inside, there wasn't really all of them, just all the documents thrown in,

00:23:23.560 --> 00:23:25.560
but it was classified according to projects.

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:28.560
So just pick a box and then show.

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:31.560
And often we would also look at his notebooks with his amazing drawings.

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:34.560
That would be another form of walk, of stroll.

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:38.560
I would then finally get him focused on the Serge Anson's exhibition that morning.

00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:43.560
We would go from the umbrella to the rain to the Serge Anson's museum.

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:46.560
And I remember he told me that what he loved about the Serge Anson's museum

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:50.820
that it used to be that during rainy days people were not allowed to enter

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:55.394
because the museum didn't want people to bring the rain with the shoes wet into the

00:23:55.394 --> 00:23:55.780
museum.

00:23:55.780 --> 00:23:58.200
So on rainy or snowy days the museum was closed.

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:02.480
It's a very kind of eccentric way of defining opening hours of the museum.

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:04.200
But that was true until quite recently.

00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:06.960
So we entered the Zones Museum, it was about the rain,

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:09.080
and he then had this wonderful, wonderful idea.

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:12.280
He said, you know, if you look at this museum it's more like a conversation piece

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:15.360
because when you go to a big museum, and that's the great thing about smaller

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:15.680
museums,

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:19.588
It's also so exciting, we have this conversation today here at the Serpentine

00:24:19.588 --> 00:24:20.400
Gallery, and I'm

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:23.871
co-director here at the Serpentine Gallery, and Julia Paton-Jones is director, and

00:24:23.871 --> 00:24:24.040
very

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:27.928
often when Julia and I talk, you know, this is a former tea house, and we find it

00:24:27.928 --> 00:24:28.120
very

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:31.433
interesting that actually it's a place, a house museum, or the scale of a house,

00:24:31.433 --> 00:24:31.640
leads

00:24:31.640 --> 00:24:35.540
to conversations, much more than a monumental place, it's got a threshold.

00:24:35.540 --> 00:24:39.297
And so I think Cedric was interested in this idea that the Serge Anson's museum,

00:24:39.297 --> 00:24:39.720
more than

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:43.453
any other museum, is a conversation piece, and when people enter the museum they

00:24:43.453 --> 00:24:43.640
have

00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:45.440
a conversation with the guard at the entrance.

00:24:45.440 --> 00:24:49.445
So Zedek said all of these guards will have great stories, stories of visitors,

00:24:49.445 --> 00:24:49.800
stories

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:50.940
with objects.

00:24:50.940 --> 00:24:55.540
So he basically had me interview every single guard and write down the stories and

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:55.820
bring

00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:56.820
it to him.

00:24:56.820 --> 00:25:02.163
And then he made a drawing for each single of these guards and the drawing was made

00:25:02.163 --> 00:25:02.420
into

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:07.640
a badge and they had to wear or could wear if they wanted their own story.

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:10.640
So his piece in the exhibition was a trigger for conversations.

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:12.020
It was such a delight.

00:25:12.020 --> 00:25:16.880
He basically gave to each guard their own story drawn, and they of course all loved

00:25:16.880 --> 00:25:18.340
it and put it on.

00:25:18.340 --> 00:25:21.914
And obviously that prompted the viewer then to ask the guard, what's that thing

00:25:21.914 --> 00:25:22.140
here?

00:25:22.140 --> 00:25:25.340
And then the guard could tell the story, and would each time tell a different story.

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:27.140
I mean, it's a fantastic invention.

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:32.064
The question of what is relevant about Cedric cannot be answered in one answer,

00:25:32.064 --> 00:25:32.500
because

00:25:32.500 --> 00:25:34.340
I think there are so many dimensions to Cedric.

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:36.140
It's almost like superstring theory.

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:38.900
I mean, I think one, and superstring theory has 11 dimensions.

00:25:38.900 --> 00:25:46.030
I think one of the dimensions is certainly this idea of the fun palace, of a

00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:46.780
cultural

00:25:46.780 --> 00:25:52.566
institution where he said that it's in a way a laboratory, yet it also serves as a

00:25:52.566 --> 00:25:53.060
harvest

00:25:53.060 --> 00:25:54.060
for the quiet eye.

00:25:54.060 --> 00:25:55.060
It's slow and fast.

00:25:55.060 --> 00:25:57.200
It sort of produces difference.

00:25:57.200 --> 00:26:01.180
It's an engine for difference in a maybe more and more homogenized culture.

00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:05.980
So in a way, I think that is something which is very relevant for the 21st century.

00:26:05.980 --> 00:26:11.400
I think what is also very relevant for the 21st century are his ideas of the school.

00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:16.754
I think the idea of the Potteries Thinkbelt as a kind of a new possibility of

00:26:16.754 --> 00:26:17.380
injecting

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:23.786
basically a school which remains physical but which can happen sort of in between

00:26:23.786 --> 00:26:24.340
remains

00:26:24.340 --> 00:26:25.920
a great model, I think.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:27.460
And we could go through all his projects.

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:30.020
I also think that the pop-up parliament should happen one day.

00:26:30.020 --> 00:26:33.944
I mean, it's this idea that we have lots of institutions which are only used once a

00:26:33.944 --> 00:26:34.180
month

00:26:34.180 --> 00:26:36.180
or once a week, but which are there all the time.

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:39.180
So if these institutions would only pop up when they're needed,

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:43.180
and they would not be there, you know, it's sort of interesting as well.

00:26:43.180 --> 00:26:47.680
I think what is also a very important part of his legacy is this last project for

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:48.180
New York,

00:26:48.180 --> 00:26:52.180
which I think needs to be emphasized more because it's such a visionary project

00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:57.180
where he decided to do nothing, where he just defined this empty space

00:26:57.180 --> 00:27:00.180
as a kind of space which should, you know, where we should not build,

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:04.218
where nothing should be built, where it would be almost like injecting oxygen into a

00:27:04.218 --> 00:27:05.180
city like Manhattan.

00:27:05.180 --> 00:27:10.404
And I think that obviously given the fact that we run out of oxygen in our

00:27:10.404 --> 00:27:11.180
metropolis,

00:27:11.180 --> 00:27:13.180
is a very important project.

00:27:13.180 --> 00:27:19.571
I think Cedric's legacy are these thousands of drawings which each single of them is

00:27:19.571 --> 00:27:20.180
a spark.

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:25.990
And I think who knows what they will trigger in the future when young architects or

00:27:25.990 --> 00:27:27.180
artists see them.

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:30.590
I think also at the moment when drawing, when Marmora's drawing happens on the

00:27:30.590 --> 00:27:30.940
internet

00:27:30.940 --> 00:27:34.448
and happens on the computer, I think his sort of doodling activity is a very

00:27:34.448 --> 00:27:35.140
important thing

00:27:35.140 --> 00:27:37.260
because you know handwriting risks to disappear.

00:27:37.260 --> 00:27:42.708
I think Cedric's entire work is about the hand drawn kind of sketches and all of

00:27:42.708 --> 00:27:42.980
that

00:27:42.980 --> 00:27:47.220
and so I think that's another very important kind of you know legacy.

00:27:47.220 --> 00:27:52.260
I think also his idea that we don't necessarily have to add object is important.

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:56.900
I mean if Tino Segal talks about this idea that art you know and it's similarly true

00:27:56.900 --> 00:28:02.811
was for architecture from the 19th century to the 1960s of the 20th century, you

00:28:02.811 --> 00:28:03.180
know,

00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:07.840
had sort of this evolutive history, but it was always connected to objects. And then

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:11.200
in the 60s you've got conceptual art, you've got a questioning of the object. I

00:28:11.200 --> 00:28:11.540
think in

00:28:11.540 --> 00:28:15.899
a way Cedric, parallel to conceptual art, you know, in the art world started to

00:28:15.899 --> 00:28:16.340
question

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:21.420
the object in architecture. And I think today there's a whole other kind of

00:28:21.420 --> 00:28:22.300
necessity for

00:28:22.300 --> 00:28:26.760
that because we live in a world where we realize more and more that resources are

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:27.200
limited.

00:28:27.200 --> 00:28:32.330
We realize more and more that there is a danger of extinction if we are not more

00:28:32.330 --> 00:28:33.100
careful with

00:28:33.100 --> 00:28:37.230
our resources and with our environment, which is why this year we are doing with

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:37.540
Gustav

00:28:37.540 --> 00:28:38.860
Messer the Extinction Marathon.

00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:41.860
I think in a way Cedric is renegotiating.

00:28:41.860 --> 00:28:44.060
The Magnet City is a very homoeopathic project.

00:28:44.060 --> 00:28:45.060
It's very 21st century.

00:28:45.060 --> 00:28:50.249
It is our idea that we can actually recharge the batteries, that we can recharge the

00:28:50.249 --> 00:28:50.620
cities

00:28:50.620 --> 00:28:53.900
without necessarily wasting energies and wasting resources.

00:28:53.900 --> 00:28:56.660
So it's very much, I think Cedric's practice in this sense,

00:28:56.660 --> 00:28:59.420
you know, connects, even if he grew out very much

00:28:59.420 --> 00:29:02.900
out of the optimism also of the 60s, you know,

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:05.720
it connects also very, very much to the current moment

00:29:05.720 --> 00:29:07.800
where we are realizing, you know,

00:29:09.020 --> 00:29:12.880
the big, big danger of unlimited growth.

00:29:12.880 --> 00:29:15.000
And Binswanger as an economist

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:16.400
thinking about the limits of growth.

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:18.560
So I think, yeah, the 21st century

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:20.020
is a Cedric Price century.
